      <!-- Section 6: Enterprise Code Generation Standard -->
      <section class="mcp-section">
        <div class="section-tag">Quality Standards</div>
        <h2 class="section-heading">Enterprise Code Generation Standard</h2>
        <p class="section-lead">
          Generic REST-to-MCP tools output brittle, insecure code that fails in production. RapiDoc enforces strict enterprise standards across every generated server:
        </p>

        <div class="standards-grid">
          <div class="standard-card">
            <div class="standard-badge">Security Invariant</div>
            <h4>1. Path Traversal & Injection Immunity</h4>
            <p>
              Generic generators use naive string interpolation (e.g. <code>fmt.Sprintf("/items/%s", id)</code>), leaving servers vulnerable to path traversal (<code>../../admin</code>). RapiDoc enforces language-native RFC 3986 percent-encoding across all path parameters in all 6 runtimes:
            </p>
            <CodeBlock code={pathEscapingSnippet} lang="go" filename="Path Escaping Parity" />
          </div>

          <div class="standard-card">
            <div class="standard-badge">Architecture Invariant</div>
            <h4>2. Dual-Mode Multi-Tenancy (Stdio + Gateway)</h4>
            <p>
              When running locally in Claude Desktop, servers read the host environment (<code>process.env.API_KEY</code>). But when deployed behind a multi-tenant agent gateway, the client must supply credentials dynamically. RapiDoc generates dual-mode auth resolution:
            </p>
            <CodeBlock code={dualModeAuthSnippet} lang="typescript" filename="Dual-Mode Auth Resolution" />
          </div>

          <div class="standard-card">
            <div class="standard-badge">RFC 6570 Compliance</div>
            <h4>3. Dynamic Resource Query Forwarding & Secret Scrubbing</h4>
            <p>
              When agents access an MCP Resource with URI query parameters (e.g., <code>reference://orders?status=shipped&apiKey=secret</code>), RapiDoc cleanly parses query bounds, preserves parameter filters, and scrubs sensitive API keys before dispatching upstream requests.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="standard-card">
            <div class="standard-badge">Zero-Dependency Runtimes</div>
            <h4>4. Lightweight Runtimes & Instant Cold Starts</h4>
            <p>
              Generated servers avoid heavy third-party networking bloat. Python uses standard library <code>urllib</code>; Go uses native <code>net/http</code> to compile into a single static binary with <strong>&lt;15ms startup</strong>; Rust compiles with tokio into an ultra-lean <strong>&lt;10 MB RAM</strong> footprint.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

